const title = "Rooms & Exits Chapter 4 Level 3 Walkthrough: The Office";
const description = "Rooms & Exits Chapter 4 Level 3 The Office walkthrough: the stained paper and the USB drive, the three posters whose glowing digits read 7 3 6, the running wall clock, the red valve handle that steams up the bathroom mirror, the four-slot Greek laptop, the three paintings, the credenza's three-dial lock, the dead laptop that needs a charger, the jigsaw whose printed photo reads 7205, and the four-dial lock that ends the level. Every printed number was read twice from separate frames.";

const faq = [
  {
    q: "What is the code for the four-dial lock at the end of The Office?",
    a: "It is <b>7205</b>. Three independent reads agree: the printed photo shows <b>7 2 0 5</b> across its lower right at 6:07, the four-dial lock is turned to <b>7-2-0-4</b> at 6:32 and <b>7-2-0-5</b> at 6:36, and the drawer opens a second later. Inside is a <b>silver key</b>, which the run then uses on the exit door.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where does 7205 come from?",
    a: "The jigsaw. A full-screen jigsaw mini-game runs from about <b>4:40</b> to <b>6:04</b>, and its reward is a <b>Printed photo</b> — the finished autumn-garden picture with digits stamped on it. The photo is the only place the four digits appear together, so do not try to derive them from the pie charts or the wall clock: neither of those produced a readable number in any frame we pulled.",
  },
  {
    q: "What do the three posters say and what are the glowing digits?",
    a: "The three motivational posters hang side by side on the meeting room's blue wall: <b>the time is now</b>, <b>DONE is better than PERFECT</b>, <b>IF THE PLAN DOESN'T WORK CHANGE THE PLAN BUT NEVER THE GOAL</b>. Each has one digit burning white where a letter should be: <b>7</b> after \"now\", <b>3</b> in place of the O in \"DONE\", and <b>6</b> in place of the O in \"WORK\". Left to right that is <b>7 3 6</b> — three separate reads of the close-up agree.",
  },
  {
    q: "So is the three-dial lock code 736?",
    a: "We will not tell you that, and here is why: the posters read <b>7 3 6</b> left to right, but the dials in the recording finish at <b>3 6 7</b> at 4:05 and the drawer is never shown open. Our two reads of the final dial positions disagree about the last digit (1 then 7 then 8 across three frames), so the lock's accepted combination is not something this page can print. The three digits you have are <b>3, 6 and 7</b>; the order is the part that never resolved.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is on the bathroom mirror?",
    a: "Four Greek letters wiped clear in the condensation above the sink, left to right. Our reads did not agree on the exact glyphs — three passes came back as <b>Ζ Ψ Ω Λ</b>, <b>Ψ ω λ</b> and <b>ζ ψ ω λ</b> — so this page tells you the mirror is the clue and stops there rather than printing a sequence you would have to trust on faith. The letters are letter-shaped, not numbers: they are what the laptop wants.",
  },
  {
    q: "How do I get the bathroom mirror to steam up?",
    a: "Take the red <b>Valve handle</b> in the bathroom (1:18) and fit it to the <b>red pipe</b> beside the shelving unit (1:22). The pipes are hot and cold — red and blue — and the valve wheel is missing when you arrive. Once the handle is on and turned, the mirror above the vanity carries the letters. The run also picks up a <b>Paint thinner</b> in this room at 1:15, before the handle.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does the laptop in the office want?",
    a: "Four Greek letters. The laptop opens a white window with <b>four slots</b>, each holding one letter, with an up and a down arrow under every slot to cycle through the alphabet. Set them to the four letters from the steamy mirror, left to right. When the slots are right the window closes and the desktop appears with a single folder-and-magnifier icon at <b>2:00</b>.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the big screen with pie charts for?",
    a: "Unknown, and we are not going to invent it. The meeting room's wall screen shows <b>three pie charts</b> with numbers in the slices, plus two diagonal stripes behind them. Three reads of that screen produced three different slice values, so none of it is printed here as a code. Treat the screen as scenery until you have a lock that refuses everything else.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the keypad on the door?",
    a: "A numeric keypad set into a wooden door, with a display above the digits and a clear key and an enter key under them. The run opens it at <b>2:24</b> and works it, but the display is masked in every frame we pulled, so the door code is not on this page. Whatever it is, it is not 7205 — that one belongs to the drawer at the end of the level.",
  },
  {
    q: "What do the cloth and the paint thinner make?",
    a: "A <b>Cloth with thinner</b>. Both arrive separately — the <b>Paint thinner</b> in the bathroom at 1:15 and the <b>Cloth</b> at 2:29 — and at <b>2:33</b> the inventory shows them selected together with Combine lit up. The game names the result <b>Cloth with thinner</b> and the run carries it from there, but no frame shows what it is wiped on, so this page does not print a target.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the story with the second decorative cube?",
    a: "There are two. The first is a pickup in the office at <b>0:52</b> — a glass cube with a wooden frame and a red and a purple flower inside. By <b>4:12</b> the inventory holds two cubes, the second with yellow and purple inside, right after the credenza's three-dial lock sequence. The drawer is never shown open, so all we can say is the order: lock first, second cube after.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the recording show the level being completed?",
    a: "Yes. At <b>6:38</b> the four-dial lock opens onto a silver key, the key goes on the exit door at 6:41, and at <b>6:44</b> the game cuts to the <b>Reward!</b> card: <b>+50</b> coins and <b>Time 11:04</b>, with the piggy-bank panel underneath. We do not print the efficiency figure — see the last section of this page.",
  },
  {
    q: "Where does The Office sit in Chapter 4?",
    a: "Chapter 4 is <i>Pharaoh's Bride</i> and it has <b>15 rooms</b>. The Office is room <b>3 of 15</b>, straight after <a href=\"/rooms-and-exits/chapter-4-level-2/\">Power and Security</a> and before Planetarium. It is the chapter's loosest level so far: instead of one machine to repair, it is a floor of rooms — office, restroom, meeting room — with a dozen small locks scattered across them.",
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "") },
  })),
};
