    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-office.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 4 Level 3 The Office: an open-plan office with an L-shaped desk, a printer and laptop, three framed abstract paintings over a two-chair waiting area, a tall bookshelf, a restroom door on the left and a green EXIT sign over the door on the right" width="1200" height="776" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The office is the room you spawn in and the one you keep coming back to. It is an open-plan corner: an L-shaped dark desk with a printer, a laptop, a coffee cup and loose papers on it, office chairs around it, a tall bookshelf against the far wall, a two-chair waiting area on a blue rug, and three small abstract paintings over it lit by individual spotlights. Two doors matter — the one on the left wall with a two-figure restroom sign, and the one on the right under a green EXIT sign.</p>

    <p>The bathroom behind the restroom door is the level's workshop. Tiled floor, a wooden vanity with a round basin and a big mirror over it, a toilet, folded towels, a wall of toiletries on an open shelving unit, a mop and bucket by the door, and — the part that matters — a pair of exposed pipes on the wall, one <b>red</b> and one <b>blue</b>, with the valve wheel missing off the red one. The mirror is clear when you walk in. It is not clear when you leave.</p>

    <p>The meeting room is where the level's numbers live. A long dark table ringed with brown office chairs, a huge wall screen showing colour bars at first and three pie charts later, and on the blue back wall the three motivational posters with a Roman-numeral clock beside them. Under the posters runs a long wooden credenza with drawers — one of which carries the three-dial lock — and on top of it sit a desk lamp, a model aeroplane and closed laptops.</p>

    <p>What holds it together is that nothing is a code for its own sake. The posters' digits exist for a lock, the mirror's letters exist for a laptop, and the jigsaw exists to produce a photo. When a number in this level has no lock behind it, that is usually a sign you have not found the lock yet.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Everything we could identify and what it is for. Timestamps are video time.</p>
