    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:06</span>
      <strong>Read the room before you touch it</strong>
      The run opens on the level card — <b>Level 3</b>, <b>The Office</b> — with Fiona and Jack trading a line about something impossible, and then the camera pulls back onto the whole floor. Take the sweep seriously: the desk with its printer and laptop, the three paintings over the waiting chairs, the bookshelf, the restroom door on the left, the EXIT door on the right. Every object this level cares about is visible from here except the bathroom and the meeting room.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-office.jpg" alt="The office in The Office with the L-shaped desk, printer and laptop, three abstract paintings over a two-chair waiting area, a tall bookshelf, the restroom door on the left and the EXIT door on the right" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=6s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:06</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:13 to 0:19 · two pickups</span>
      <strong>Stained paper, then the USB drive</strong>
      The <b>Stained paper</b> comes first, lifted off the desk with its label under it: a sheet with a large brown ring bleed and a mark inside the ring. Six seconds later the <b>USB flash drive</b> is picked up from the same cluster of desk clutter — a black stick with a metal connector, labelled in plain words. Neither is a puzzle you can solve where you find it. They are inventory items, and this level is long on inventory items.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-stained-paper.jpg" alt="The Stained paper pickup in The Office, a sheet with a large brown ring stain and a mark inside it, floating over the office desk" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-usb-drive.jpg" alt="The USB flash drive pickup in The Office, a black stick with a metal connector, labelled USB flash drive" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=13s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:13</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=19s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:19</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:21 · the three posters</span>
      <strong>7, 3 and 6 — one digit per poster</strong>
      This is the level's first real clue and it is easy to walk past. The three motivational posters on the meeting room's blue wall each have a single digit glowing white where a letter should be: the <b>7</b> sits after \"now\" on <i>the time is now</i>, the <b>3</b> replaces the O in \"DONE\", and the <b>6</b> replaces the O in \"WORK\". Left to right that is <b>7 3 6</b>. The close-up holds for more than a minute, which is your hint that it matters.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-poster-digits.jpg" alt="The three inspirational posters in The Office with glowing digits: the time is now with a 7, DONE is better than PERFECT with a 3, and IF THE PLAN DOESN'T WORK with a 6" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=21s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:21</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:25 to 0:49 · the clock</span>
      <strong>A clock that runs, and nothing to set</strong>
      The run spends nearly half a minute on the Roman-numeral wall clock beside the posters, and the hands are moving the whole time — frame differencing on the dial puts the movement in a single hand sweeping just past the hour. What we could not do is pin a reading: the same frame came back as \"just past II\" and as \"short hand on III\" on two different passes, and the dial's hands never hold still long enough to be sure. So treat the clock as a running clock, not as a dial you can set. If a lock later wants a time, it is not this one.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-wall-clock.jpg" alt="The Roman-numeral wall clock in The Office, a white dial floating in front of the blue wall with the posters to its right" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=25s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:25</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=45s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:45</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 0:52 · the decorative cube</span>
      <strong>A glass cube with two flowers inside</strong>
      Next to the posters hangs a small <b>Decorative cube</b>: a wooden frame around glass panels, and inside it a red flower and a purple one. It is labelled and collected at 0:52. Do not read anything into it yet — the run does not use it for a while, and by <b>4:12</b> the inventory will hold <b>two</b> of them, the second with yellow and purple flowers inside.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-decorative-cube.jpg" alt="The Decorative cube in The Office, a wooden-framed glass cube holding a red and a purple flower" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=52s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:52</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:15 to 1:22 · the bathroom</span>
      <strong>Paint thinner, then a valve wheel for the red pipe</strong>
      Through the restroom door: tiled floor, vanity and mirror, toilet, a wall of toiletries on open shelving, a mop and bucket by the door, and two vertical pipes — red and blue — running up the wall beside the shelves. Two pickups before you touch the pipes. The <b>Paint thinner</b> at 1:15, and the <b>Valve handle</b> at 1:18, a red three-spoke wheel with a metal hub. Fit that handle to the <b>red pipe</b> and turn it at 1:22.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-bathroom.jpg" alt="The bathroom in The Office with a wooden vanity, round basin and mirror, a toilet, open shelving of toiletries, a mop and bucket, and a red and a blue pipe on the wall" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-valve-handle.jpg" alt="The red Valve handle pickup in the bathroom of The Office, a three-spoke wheel with a silver hub" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=75s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:15</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=78s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:18</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=82s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:22</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:28 · the mirror</span>
      <strong>Four Greek letters in the steam</strong>
      With the hot pipe open the mirror over the vanity fogs, and four Greek letters are wiped clear in the condensation, left to right. This is the clue the whole rest of the level runs on: the office laptop is waiting for exactly these letters. We are not printing the sequence — three passes at the same close-up came back with different glyphs, and a wrong letter here costs you a cycle on a stepper you cannot undo quickly. Read them off your own screen; they are large enough to see.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-mirror-letters.jpg" alt="The bathroom mirror in The Office fogged with steam, with Greek letters wiped clear in the condensation above the basin" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=88s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:28</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:36 to 2:00 · the laptop</span>
      <strong>Four slots, four steppers, one letter each</strong>
      Back in the office. The desk laptop opens a white window on a purple-to-orange wallpaper with <b>four slots</b>, each holding a single Greek letter, and an <b>up and a down arrow</b> under every slot. Set them to the mirror's four letters, left to right. The run finishes at <b>2:00</b> and the window closes onto the desktop — a gradient wallpaper with one circular folder-and-magnifier icon in the middle. Nothing else on that screen is interactive in any frame we pulled.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-laptop-greek.jpg" alt="The four-slot Greek letter puzzle on the office laptop in The Office, four letter tiles above up and down arrow steppers" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=96s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:36</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=120s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:08 to 2:27 · screen and keypad</span>
      <strong>The pie charts, and a keypad you cannot read</strong>
      Two stations back to back in the meeting room. The big wall screen switches from colour bars to <b>three pie charts</b> with numbers in the slices and two diagonal stripes behind them; then the run works a <b>numeric keypad</b> set into a wooden door — digits, a clear key, an enter key, and a display above them. Neither gives us a printable answer: the pie slices changed value between reads, and the keypad display is masked every time it is on camera. Method only, no numbers.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-pie-charts.jpg" alt="The meeting room wall screen in The Office showing three pie charts with slice numbers and two diagonal stripes" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-door-keypad.jpg" alt="The numeric keypad set into a wooden door in The Office, with a display above the digits, a clear key and an enter key" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=128s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:08</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=144s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:29 to 2:35 · the cloth</span>
      <strong>Cloth plus paint thinner</strong>
      The <b>Cloth</b> arrives at 2:29 — a folded peach-coloured rag — and at <b>2:33</b> the inventory shows it selected alongside the <b>Paint thinner</b> with <b>Combine</b> lit. The result is a <b>Cloth with thinner</b>, and the run carries it around for the rest of the level. What it gets wiped on is the one thing this level never shows, so do not go hunting for a stained surface: there is no frame where the cloth touches anything.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-cloth-thinner.jpg" alt="The inventory in The Office with Cloth and Paint thinner selected together and the Combine action lit, over the office background" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=149s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:29</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=153s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:33</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 2:40 · the three paintings</span>
      <strong>One of them carries an I</strong>
      Over the waiting chairs hang three small abstract panels in white, blue and red frames, each under its own spotlight. At <b>2:40</b> they come up as a close-up and the difference is easy to spot once you look: the <b>middle</b> painting carries a black square with a white <b>I</b> near its bottom edge, and the <b>right</b> painting has an orange splat across its lower half. What the three panels feed is not visible in this run — there is no frame of them being rearranged or matched to anything.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-three-paintings.jpg" alt="The three abstract paintings in The Office on the blue wall, the middle one carrying a black square with a white letter I and the right one with an orange splash" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=160s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:40</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 3:52 to 4:06 · the credenza lock</span>
      <strong>Three dials, and a code we will not print</strong>
      The credenza under the posters has a drawer with a <b>three-dial combination lock</b> — three vertical columns of digits with an indicator over each. The run works it from 3:52 and finishes with the dials reading <b>3 6 7</b> at 4:05. That is the same three digits as the posters, in a different order, and we cannot tell you which order the game accepts: the dials pass through 3-6-1, 3-6-8 and 3-6-7 across three frames, and the drawer is never shown open. Start from the posters' own reading and if it refuses, rotate.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-three-digit-lock.jpg" alt="The three-dial combination lock on the meeting room credenza in The Office, three columns of digits with selection indicators above" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=232s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:52</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=245s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:05</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 13 · 4:29 to 4:39 · the dead laptop</span>
      <strong>Find the charger, then wait for the battery</strong>
      The second laptop is flat when the run reaches it — a black screen behind the focus brackets. The fix is an item, not a puzzle: a <b>charger</b> arrives at 4:29, gets plugged into the laptop, and by <b>4:36</b> the screen shows nothing but a battery glyph with a green bolt through it and a plug on a lead beside it. There is an orange sticky note on the bezel too, but its handwriting is not legible at our resolution and this page does not print it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-laptop-charging.jpg" alt="The second laptop in The Office showing a charging battery graphic with a green lightning bolt and a plug, with an orange sticky note on the bezel" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=269s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:29</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=276s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:36</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 14 · 4:40 to 6:04 · the jigsaw</span>
      <strong>An autumn garden, one loose piece at a time</strong>
      The level's longest single station: a jigsaw of a Japanese autumn garden — red maple, a pagoda roof, an arched bridge over water, a full moon in the upper left — laid out on a dark green board. Pieces are dragged into place one at a time and the run spends nearly <b>eighty-five seconds</b> on it, which is the honest answer to &quot;why is this level eleven minutes on the clock&quot;. Finish it and the board clears.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-jigsaw.jpg" alt="The full-screen jigsaw mini-game in The Office, an autumn Japanese garden with red maple, a pagoda, a bridge and a full moon on a dark green board" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=280s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:40</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=364s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:04</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 15 · 6:07 · the printed photo</span>
      <strong>7 2 0 5</strong>
      The jigsaw's reward is a <b>Printed photo</b>: the finished garden picture with four white digits stamped along its lower right edge, <b>7 2 0 5</b>. It floats over the desk and is collected at 6:07. This is the only place in the level where those four digits appear together, and it is the code for the very next lock — so take the photo, do not just tap past it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-printed-photo.jpg" alt="The Printed photo reward in The Office, the autumn garden picture with the digits 7 2 0 5 stamped along its lower right edge" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=367s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:07</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 16 · 6:24 to 6:46 · the last lock and the exit</span>
      <strong>7205, a silver key, and a low-efficiency finish</strong>
      The last lock is a <b>four-dial</b> version on a wooden drawer front. Turn it to <b>7-2-0-5</b> and it opens at 6:38 onto a <b>silver key</b>; the key goes straight onto the exit door at 6:41. Then the <b>Reward!</b> card: <b>+50</b> coins and <b>Time 11:04</b>, with a ten-segment efficiency bar and the piggy-bank panel underneath. The run's clock is long for this level and that is the honest reason: it wanders, and the jigsaw alone eats a minute and a half of it.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-four-digit-lock.jpg" alt="The four-dial combination lock in The Office turned to 7205, set into a wooden drawer front" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c4l3-level-complete.jpg" alt="The Reward screen for The Office in Rooms and Exits showing plus 50 coins, a ten-segment efficiency bar and Time 11:04" width="1200" height="776" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=384s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:24</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=398s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:38</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=agedEZeIoG4&t=404s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">6:44</a></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>You try to read the wall clock.</b> It is the most prominent object in the level and the camera sits on it for twenty-five seconds, so it feels like a puzzle. It is a running clock: the hand keeps moving the whole time and never matches itself between passes. Nothing in this run sets it, and the only lock that wants a number gets it from the photo.</p>
    <p><b>You assume the posters' order is the lock's order.</b> The posters read <b>7 3 6</b> left to right. The dials finish on <b>3 6 7</b>. Both are real reads of the same three digits, which is exactly why this page refuses to name a combination for the credenza drawer — the order is the part nobody can confirm from the video.</p>
    <p><b>You skip the bathroom.</b> The mirror is the only source for the laptop code and the valve handle is sitting right next to the pipe it belongs on. If you reach the laptop with the four slots blank and nothing to put in them, you have not been through the restroom door yet.</p>
    <p><b>You look for a purpose for the cloth with thinner.</b> There isn't one visible. The combine is real, the item is real, and no frame ever shows it applied to anything. It may be used in a later room, or it may be this level's leftover — either way, do not burn time hunting for a dirty surface.</p>
    <p><b>You miss the second laptop.</b> It is dead on arrival and the only fix is the charger pickup, which the run collects without ceremony at 4:29. If a jigsaw never appears for you, that is the step you skipped — the jigsaw is what the laptop opens onto.</p>

    <h2>Where The Office sits in Chapter 4<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 4 is <i>Pharaoh's Bride</i> and it runs <b>15 rooms</b>. The Office is room <b>3 of 15</b>, between <a href="/rooms-and-exits/chapter-4-level-2/">Power and Security</a> and Planetarium. Where the first two rooms were single machines with a clear repair arc, this one is a floor of small locks — a scavenger level rather than an engineering one.</p>
    <p>It is also the chapter's first real inventory level. By 4:46 you are carrying a stained paper, a USB drive, two decorative cubes, a cloth soaked in thinner, a neon light, a wire and a charger, and the game is content to let most of that sit there. Do not read a big design behind every item. The ones that matter are the ones with a lock behind them, and this page points at each of those.</p>

