    <h2>What this page could not verify<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <div class="warn">
      Six things, stated plainly so you know which parts of this page are reads and which are reconstruction.
      <br><br>
      <b>1. The four letters on the bathroom mirror.</b> This is the level's most important clue and it is also the one that would not settle. Three passes at the same close-up returned <b>Ζ Ψ Ω Λ</b>, <b>Ψ ω λ</b> and <b>ζ ψ ω λ</b> — the glyph identities agree roughly but the count and the case do not. Printing a four-letter sequence that the player will type into a stepper with no undo would be worse than printing nothing, so the page points at the mirror and stops.
      <br><br>
      <b>2. The credenza's three-dial combination.</b> The dials pass through 3-6-1, 3-6-8 and 3-6-7 in three consecutive frames of the same close-up, the drawer is never shown open, and the posters' own order is 7-3-6. Two readings of the same three digits in two different orders is exactly the case where we do not print a code. The digits are 3, 6 and 7; the order is yours to test.
      <br><br>
      <b>3. The pie-chart slices and the door keypad.</b> The wall screen's three charts have numbers in the slices, but three reads produced three different values, and the keypad's display is masked in every frame it appears in. Neither is printed as a code, and neither is needed: the numbers this level actually uses come from the posters and the photo.
      <br><br>
      <b>4. What the cloth with thinner, the second decorative cube, the neon light and the wire are for.</b> All four are real pickups with real labels — the run is carrying them by 4:46 — and none of them is ever used on camera. They may belong to a later room, or to a puzzle this recording skips. The page lists them as items and says nothing about a target.
      <br><br>
      <b>5. The efficiency score on the Reward card.</b> There is a ten-segment bar under the word &quot;Efficiency&quot; and we could not count it the same way twice — three passes at the same close-up came back as <b>7</b>, <b>8</b> and <b>10</b> segments filled, and a fourth pass counted a bar on the panel below at <b>4</b>. The bar is small and the card only holds for a couple of seconds. It is worth nothing to you as a player, so it is not printed. <b>+50</b> and <b>11:04</b> on the same card are stable and are printed above.
      <br><br>
      <b>6. The sticky note on the laptop's bezel.</b> There is an orange sticky note with handwriting on it, and the handwriting is the kind of thing a guide would love to print. Three crops of it read <b>9:35 am</b>, <b>Call John @ 3pm</b> and <b>. 5 am</b>. That is a hallucination test, not a clue — so the note is described and not transcribed.
